{
  "rules": [
    "Uptrend underestimation: When the last 5–10 days show a clear upward drift, the model is systematically too low versus realized levels. Apply a positive level shift of ~+1.5 to +3.0 EUR early in the horizon (days 1–20), tapering toward ~0 by days 25–30.",
    "Turning-point lag: The forecast path tends to react with delay to regime changes (especially transitions into rallies). If momentum (e.g., 5d return) turns positive after a flat/down period, steepen the first half of the forecast (increase day-to-day slope) rather than just shifting the level.",
    "Peak/late-horizon overstay: After strong rallies, the model often keeps prices elevated too long and misses subsequent sell-offs into the far horizon. Add stronger mean-reversion in days 20–30 (pull forecasts toward the 30d history mean/last price), especially if recent volatility/spike is high.",
    "Spike handling asymmetry: The model underpredicts upside spikes (true outcomes jump higher than forecast) but can overpredict persistence afterward. Heuristic: allow higher short-term amplitude (raise near-term highs) but enforce faster decay back toward trend/mean beyond ~2 weeks.",
    "Horizon-specific calibration: Net bias is positive in the near term during rallies (needs upward correction), but becomes neutral-to-negative in the far tail after big moves (needs downward/mean-reverting correction). Implement a time-varying bias: +b for days 1–15, 0 for days 16–20, −b/2 (or mean-reversion pull) for days 21–30 when prior 10d trend is strongly positive."
  ]
}
